# Learning Objectives

@body

- Compare sex determination in fruit flies and humans.
- Compare dosage compensation in worms, flies, and mammals.
- Relate X inactivation to Barr bodies and mosaic traits.
- Predict sex-chromosome phenotypes in the quiz examples.

=== layout: section

# Review of Sex Determination in Drosophila

=== layout: one-panel

# *Drosophila Sex determination*

@body

- XX = wildtype fertile female
- XY = wildtype fertile male
- Same as humans...

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *Drosophila Sex determination*

@left

- Drosophila
- [XX = normal fertile female]{color=red}
- [XY = normal fertile male]{color=blue}
- [XXY = normal fertile female]{color=red}
- [X0 = normal sterile male]{color=blue}
- So Y does not factor into maleness.

@right

- Humans
- [XX = normal fertile female]{color=red}
- [XY = normal fertile male]{color=blue}
- [XXY = male with female features]{color=blue}
- [X0 = sterile female]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Genetic control of sex determination in Drosophila*

@body

- The X-chromosome/autosome ratio controls the switching on/off of the master control gene _sex-lethal_ (_Sxl_)

![Slide image 1](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_001.jpg)

- activity of the Sex-lethal gene _Sxl_.

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *Sex determination*

@left

- X = sex chromosome
- A = autosomes
- XXAA ratio of 1
  - Diploid with 2 X
  - normal female
- XAA ratio of &#189;
  - Diploid with 1 X and 1 Y
  - normal male
- XXAAA ratio of 2/3
  - Triploid with 2 X
  - intersex
- XA ratio of 1
  - Haploid with 1 X
  - initially female then dies

@right

- Hypothesis\:
  - ratio of 1 is female
  - ratio of &#189; is male
  - ratio in between is intersex

=== layout: section

# Dosage Compensation

=== layout: one-panel

# *Dosage Compensation*

@body

- For organisms with XY sex determination, there is a problem with dosage of genes on the X and Y.
  - Dosage compensation has evolved to correct the unequal dosage in the sexes.
- [*Dosage Compensation*]{underline=true} describes the process organisms use to equalize the expression of genes between different biological sexes.

=== layout: two-panels

# *C. elegans = nematode worms*

@left

![Slide image 2](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_002.png)

@right

- In females, each X chromosome expression is reduced by half.
- Both X's have the same activity levels

=== layout: two-panels

# *Drosophila = fruit fly*

@left

- In males, a protein complex on the Y, increases transcriptional activity of the X.
- The single male X does twice the work to equal the XX of the female.

@right

![Slide image 3](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_003.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# *X Inactivation in Mammals*

@left

- In mammalian females, one of the two X chromosomes in each cell is randomly inactivated during embryonic development
  - The inactive X condenses into a *Barr body*
- Normally, the inactivated X is random.
  - A single organism will have different inactive X's throughout their body.
  - Heterozygous genes on the X chromosome, will produce a mosaic for that character.
  - [_Marsupials always deactivate the paternal X._]{color=red}

@right

![Slide image 4](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_004.png)

